INT. NIGHT-SHIFT PLANETARIUM - CONTROL BOOTH - NIGHT

A cramped booth above the dome. Dials, slide trays, a dusty reel-to-reel, and a view through a narrow window onto rows of empty reclining seats. Overhead, painted stars crawl across the curved ceiling in slow rotation.

ADAEZE "ADA" LINDQVIST, 30s, planetarium technician, headphones around her neck and a thermos at her elbow, sits hunched at the console. She types a note. Deletes it. Types again.

A phone sits beside the keyboard. Its screen is lit, a call connected, the speaker on.

                    RADIO ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
(over speaker)
...and that was Chet Baker, here on KLUN, three in the morning, and still nobody's asleep.

Ada reaches over and kills the radio. Silence settles on the booth.

She exhales. Picks up the phone. Looks at the call screen. It reads: NOAH, 02:51.

She doesn't speak. Neither does he.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
Ada?

                    ADA
Hi.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
You're awake.

                    ADA
I work nights.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
I know. I just thought...

                    ADA
You thought what?

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
I didn't know you'd pick up.

                    ADA
Neither did I.

She laughs, small and wet, and wipes her eye with a knuckle. On the dome behind her, Orion rises and slides toward the horizon.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
Are you somewhere quiet?

                    ADA
I'm in a room with eleven thousand stars.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
That sounds nice.

                    ADA
They're painted. It's a lie, really. I come in every night and turn the lie on.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
It's a beautiful lie.

                    ADA
Noah.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
Yes.

                    ADA
Why are you calling me?

A long silence. The reel-to-reel ticks. Somewhere in the building, a pipe knocks twice.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
Because I looked up tonight. First time in a year. And I thought of you.

She presses her knuckles to her mouth. Reaches for the dimmer, brings the dome lights down until the booth is nearly black and only the stars remain.

                    ADA
Where are you?

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
On a roof. In Lisbon.

                    ADA
Why Lisbon?

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
I got a job. I didn't tell you.

                    ADA
(quiet)
You didn't.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
I didn't know how to.

                    ADA
You could have called.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
I did. Just now.

She laughs again, and this time it breaks into something else. She turns her face to the window, away from the receiver.

                    ADA
You can see the stars from there?

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
Barely. Too many lights.

                    ADA
Then you're missing them.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
I'm missing something.

Ada reaches out and flips a switch on the console. The dome shifts. The painted sky races backward through the hours, winding through the night like a film in reverse. Constellations spin and tumble.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
What are you doing?

                    ADA
Turning the sky.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
To where?

                    ADA
To where you are. Look up.

On the dome, a handful of stars pause. Hold. The booth is silent but for breath on the line.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
I'm looking.

                    ADA
Third from the left. Over the chimney.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
That one?

                    ADA
That one's me.

A long pause. His breath catches.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
I see you.

She closes her eyes. The dome's light pools across her face. For a moment she is utterly still.

                    ADA
(whispering)
Stay on the line.

                    NOAH (V.O.)
(over phone)
All night.

She leans back in the chair, phone against her ear. Above her, the lie keeps turning, softly, toward morning.
